course: dictionary
lesson: 1983
title: Compound words and compound meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Compound means made of two or more parts joined together. The word can change meaning depending on the sentence, so context tells you which meaning is meant. Look at the words around it first.
explain: «A compound word is two words joined into one.» Football is made from foot and ball. Bookkeeper is made from book and keeper. Each part still has its own meaning, and together they make a new word.
explain: In maths, «Interest that grows on interest is called compound interest.» At 10%, £100 becomes £110 after one year. In the second year the extra £10 earns £1 too, so the total is £121. The longer you wait, the faster it grows.
explain: Science, the prison, and maths all use the word differently. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: A compound word is two or more words joined into one, such as «football».
fact: A compound sentence is a sentence made of several parts joined together.
fact: A chemical compound is a substance made of two or more elements joined together. Water is a compound of hydrogen and oxygen.
fact: To compound a problem means to make it worse: «the cold compounded our troubles».
fact: «A prison compound» is an area of buildings surrounded by a fence or wall.
exercise: Which word is a compound word, made by joining two whole words? | reading | *bookkeeper | bookish | keeping
exercise: «Water is a compound of hydrogen and oxygen.» What does compound mean in this sentence? | A problem made worse | *A substance made of two or more elements joined together | A fenced area with buildings | A short sentence
exercise: «The cold compounded our troubles.» What does compounded mean? | Hid the problem | Solved the problem | Counted the problems | *Made the problem worse
test: After two years of compound interest at 10%, how much does £100 become? | £120 | £130 | £110 | *£121
test: What is a compound shape? | *A shape made of smaller shapes, like rectangles, joined together | A shape with no straight sides | A shape that is always a circle | A shape drawn in ink
exam: «A prison compound» is an area of buildings surrounded by a fence or wall. In this sentence, what does compound mean? | *An area of buildings surrounded by a fence or wall | A kind of crime | A type of medicine | A very loud noise
